-### VolumeLoader
-Handles loading volume data into memory; Sectioning operates on already loaded data, partitioning it for processing [G][Link](2045-volumeloader.md).
-
-### Hardware-Assisted Volume Streaming
-Uses hardware buffers for streaming; Sectioning uses external memory for FIFO recirculation [G][Link](2119-hardware-assisted-volume-streaming.md).
-
-### Bandwidth-based Streaming Interval Calculation
-Determines streaming intervals based on bandwidth; Sectioning determines section boundaries based on memory limits [G][Link](3128-bandwidth-based-streaming-interval-calculation.md).
-
-### Chunk-based spatial partitioning
-Divides space for parallel processing; Sectioning divides space for memory management within a single pass [G][Link](3862-chunk-based-spatial-partitioning.md).
-
-### Distributed computing and load balancing
-Distributes work across nodes; Sectioning manages memory within a single node's pipeline [G][Link](5624-distributed-computing-and-load-balancing.md).
-
-### MapReduce-based Volume Rendering
-Uses MapReduce for parallel rendering; Sectioning is a hardware pipeline optimization [G][Link](5963-mapreduce-based-volume-rendering.md).
-
-### Out-of-core brick-based volume rendering
-Loads bricks from disk; Sectioning manages intermediate values in external memory during rendering [G][Link](6288-out-of-core-brick-based-volume-rendering.md).
+**Edge Cases and Pitfalls:**
+*   **Memory Ordering:** The Section Memory must strictly adhere to FIFO semantics. Values must be written and read in the exact same order to ensure the rendering algorithm perceives no difference from a continuous on-chip pipeline [S1, unofficial].
+*   **Boundary Precision:** No approximation is allowed at section boundaries. The values written to external memory must be bit-exact to those that would have been passed on-chip to prevent visual artifacts [S1, unofficial].
 
 ## Operating characteristics
-*   **Time Complexity:** No additional computational complexity; the same values are calculated, just in a different order [S1, unofficial].
-*   **Memory Complexity:** Reduces on-chip FIFO memory requirements by off-loading intermediate values to external memory [S1, unofficial].
-*   **Measured Performance:** Implemented in VolumePro with 32-voxel sections [S1, unofficial].
-*   **Strengths:** Exact numerical precision; no visual artifacts at section boundaries [S1, unofficial].
-*   **Limitations:** Requires external memory access; limited to x-direction in VolumePro [S1, unofficial].
-*   **When to use:** When on-chip FIFO memory is insufficient for the full volume width [S1, unofficial].
-*   **When not to use:** When external memory bandwidth is a bottleneck or when hardware supports sufficient on-chip memory [S1, unofficial].
+*   **Memory Usage:** Reduces on-chip FIFO memory requirements significantly by offloading intermediate states to external memory (SDRAM) [S1, unofficial].
+*   **Performance:** Introduces overhead due to external memory access latency and the need to manage the Section Memory buffer. However, it enables rendering of larger volumes than would fit in on-chip memory.
+*   **Visual Quality:** Lossless. Since the same values are calculated and passed in the same logical order (just via external memory instead of on-chip registers), there are no visual artifacts or approximation errors introduced at section boundaries [S1, unofficial].
+*   **Scalability:** Allows the rendering of datasets larger than the on-chip memory capacity, limited only by the size of the external memory and the bandwidth to it.
 
 ## Background
-Sectioning was introduced in the VolumePro real-time ray-casting system [S1, unofficial]. It was developed to address the limitations of semiconductor processes regarding on-chip memory size [S1, unofficial]. The method is detailed in the VolumePro technical report [S1, unofficial].
+Sectioning was introduced as part of the VolumePro real-time ray-casting system, specifically designed for the vg500 rendering ASIC [S1, unofficial]. The method addresses the physical limitations of semiconductor processes at the time, which restricted the amount of on-chip memory available for the FIFOs necessary for the pipeline architecture [S1, unofficial]. It is a key component of the VolumePro PCI card's ability to handle large volume datasets [S1, unofficial].
 
